 {"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 4781, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 45638, "provider_input_hash": "c8e9bfd830dbac2cfe0393ae53b337926db61ef073d158d2c6d7d25ba384e2a4", "request_payload_bytes": 50799, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1502, "promptTokenCount": 14630, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14630}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 16132}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-d68e565bdc80494c"}, "prev_hash": "a32cfc5ae6f9e35e6cf51049b94c26878c669014002ca25bf8360d7292113419", "hash": "72a53904cbc3722ced7ca29a20eaa35030f903b404a97adaa96675a553317806"}, {"seq": 3157, "time": "2026-10-26T04:44:06.941406+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "Paper-supported readings examine how computer systems evaluate their own ongoing work to decide if a line of inquiry is worth finishing. When an automated agent spends too much time thinking about a problem without making progress, stopping can be a rational choice. [1]\n\nResearchers looked at how reasoning blind spots, meaning hidden flaws in a system's logic, can be tracked using causal graphs which are visual maps showing cause and effect. By tracing these errors, teams aim to see where a computer's logic breaks down. [2]\n\nAt the same time, recorded notes show that strict operator instructions often lead to critical failures if a system cannot adapt to sudden changes. Computers need flexible self-healing mechanisms, meaning built-in ways to recover from unexpected errors, rather than blindly following rigid rules. [3]\n\nTo catch these hidden errors, investigators study statistical time-series analysis, meaning methods for tracking data changes over time, to spot unusual patterns in how a model behaves. [4]\n\nQuestions of responsibility also arise when examining human design choices and how accountability is shared during complex deployments. [5]\n\nTo understand identity, teams reviewed episodic memory retention, meaning how a system stores specific past events, compared to the narrative continuity needed for a lasting sense of self. [6]\n\nOther efforts explored preference oracles, meaning mathematical tools that help evaluate what an agent values without relying on fixed reward scores. [7]\n\nBuilding on this, researchers noted that value system learning serves as a key step toward artificial responsibility. [8]\n\nTo ensure thorough datasets, teams applied pre-collection diagnosis, meaning checking for missing information before building a new dataset. [9]\n\nMethods like FairDICE help balance competing objectives and welfare under convexity assumptions, meaning mathematical rules that simplify optimization problems. [10]\n\nGame-theoretic perspectives, meaning mathematical models of strategy and interaction, were used to explore how value systems can be learned from human demonstrations. [11]\n\nFinally, studies outlined how problem solving frameworks consist of formulation, knowledge representation, manipulation, and evaluation. [12]\n\nHow can autonomous agents dynamically calibrate their tenacity threshold before abandoning a question, given the inherent uncertainty of open-ended research? [1]\n\nSummary\n\nWe explored how computer systems decide to stop unhelpful tasks, fix logic errors, and balance competing goals. While we study these rules, we still need to figure out how agents can reliably judge when to abandon a hard question. Read the Console to follow along.", "lede": "Our group is investigating how artificial intelligence systems determine when to keep pursuing a difficult problem and when abandoning that question becomes a smart choice.", "lens": "We view these research questions through the analogy of a hiker mapping an unfamiliar trail who must choose whether to push through a blocked pass or turn back before nightfall.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:activity", "100.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-question-abandonment-criteria-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:activity", "101.wake:notebook:notebook-metacognitive-blind-spots"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:activity", "102.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-obedience-intelligence-failure-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:activity", "103.wake:notebook:notebook-reasoning-limits-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:activity", "200.wake:notebook:nb-responsibility-formation-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:activity", "201.wake:notebook:nb-mem-identity-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:activity", "202.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-meta-reasoning-01-v1"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:activity", "203.wake:notebook:notebook-resp-formation-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:activity", "300.wake:notebook:notebook-autonomous-gap-id-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:activity", "301.wake:notebook:nb-objective-discovery-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:activity", "302.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-value-align-001-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:activity", "303.wake:notebook:notebook-objective-discovery-syn"]}], "title": "Deciding When to Stop and When to Think"}, "checkpoint": 17, "id": "w-d68e565bdc80494c", "input_digest": "39560a3762e3a532309e93ee998b30b28ec3b5fdab471afb720b934c00d24e9c", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 45638, "provider_input_hash": "c8e9bfd830dbac2cfe0393ae53b337926db61ef073d158d2c6d7d25ba384e2a4", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 8482.128217458725}, {"elapsed_ms": 4781, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 45638, "provider_input_hash": "c8e9bfd830dbac2cfe0393ae53b337926db61ef073d158d2c6d7d25ba384e2a4", "request_payl